              "image_path": "images/page_13_table_9.jpg",
              "content": "<table><tr><td rowspan=\"2\">Subtracting 300 from both sides, we get <eq>28\\left (x+4  \\right ) =1000-300</eq> <eq>28\\left (x+4  \\right ) =700.</eq> Dividing both sides by 28, we get <eq>x + 4 = 700 \\div 28</eq> <eq>x + 4 = 25.</eq> So <eq>x = 2 5 - 4 ,</eq> , which gives <eq>x = 2 1 .</eq> no</td><td rowspan=\"2\"><eq>(x + 4) + 300 = 1000</eq> 28 Since 28, 300, and 1000 are divisible by 4, we get a simpler equation if we divide both sides by 4. <eq>\\frac{28(x + 4) + 300}{4} = \\frac{1000}{4}</eq> Using the rules of fraction addition, we get <eq>\\frac{28(x + 4)}{4} + \\frac{300}{4} = \\frac{1000}{4}</eq> <eq>7(x + 4) + 75 = 250</eq> Subtracting 75 from both sides, <eq>7(x + 4) = 175</eq> <eq>7x + 28 = 175</eq> Subtracting 28 from both sides, <eq>7 x = 1 4 7</eq> <eq>x = \\frac{147}{7} = 21.</eq></td><td rowspan=\"2\"><eq>28(x + 4) + 300 = 1000</eq> Simplifying the LHS, <eq>28x+112+300=1000</eq> V <eq>28x + 412 = 1000</eq> Subtracting 412 from both sides, we get <eq>28x = 1000 - 412</eq> <eq>2 8 x = 5 8 8</eq> Dividing both sides by <eq>x = 588 \\div 28</eq></td></tr><tr><td>28, <eq>x = 2 1 .</eq></td></tr></table>"
